<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kvirike III of Kakheti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1037">1037</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kvirike the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kvirike III, called the Great, was the ruler of Kakheti in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> who took the title king around 1010, creating the Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti. He defended its independence against the united Georgian monarchy of Bagrat III and his successors. After his death his kingdom passed to his nephew, and it survived until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David the Builder</a> annexed it in 1104.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kvirike-iii-of-kakheti.jpg" alt="Kvirike III of Kakheti" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brindz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Kvirike_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Kvirike succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> as prince and chorepiscopus of Kakheti, but the Bagratid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat III of Georgia</a> captured him and conquered the principality. Only after Bagrat's death in 1014 was Kvirike able to recover his crown. He then took control of the neighbouring kingdom of Hereti, declared himself King of Kakheti and Hereti, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telavi">Telavi</a> his capital and built a palace at Bodoji near Tianeti. His reign was a period of political power and prosperity for the kingdom.</p><p>In 1027 Kvirike joined a coalition against Fadhl II, the Shaddadid emir of Arran. The armies of Bagrat IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, led by Liparit Orbeliani and Ivane Abazasdze, together with Emir Jaffar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> and the Armenian King David I of Lorri, defeated the emir decisively at the Eklez River. Around 1029 Kvirike repelled an invasion by the Alan king Urdure, who had crossed the Caucasus Mountains into Kakheti and ravaged Tianeti; Urdure was killed in the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kvirike was assassinated while hunting, at the height of his power and prestige. According to the Georgian historian Vakhushti, the killer was one of the king's own Alan slaves, who sought to avenge the death of King Urdure in the war of around 1029.</p><p>Two series of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins struck under Kvirike III were found in 2012 and 2013 at Çuxur Qəbələ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and Sisian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Their design mixes Christian and Islamic elements. The obverse gives the king's name in Arabic as Abu-l'Fadl Quriqi b. Da'ud, with the shahada and the names of the Abbasid caliphs Al-Qadir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaim">Al-Qa'im</a>, a sign that Kvirike accepted Abbasid suzerainty, at least nominally, to fend off Bagratid expansion; it did not mean that he adhered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The reverse shows a horseman named in Georgian asomtavruli letters as St. George defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, the earliest known depiction of the saint on coins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kvirike III of Kakheti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1010 <b>Kvirike III</b> became chorepiscopus of the Principality of Kakheti, which had already absorbed the Principality of Hereti, and later took the title king of the Kakhs and Rans.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti">Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1010 <b>Kvirike III</b> took the title king, forming the Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti, which held out against the united Georgian monarchy until 1104.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kakheti">Principality of Kakheti</a></li></ul></section>
